                  outcome: `Captures real achievement but substantially obscures who got the vote when and what was lost. The 19th Amendment formally gave US women the vote in 1920, but Black women in the South were substantially excluded from voting until the Voting Rights Act of 1965 through systematic Jim Crow disenfranchisement (literacy tests, poll taxes, violence). The "women won the vote in 1920" framing erases the additional 45 years of disenfranchisement that Black women faced. The triumph framing also tends to obscure the racism within the suffrage movement (Stanton's and Anthony's documented racist arguments after 15th Amendment; NAWSA's substantial exclusion of Black women; the 1913 parade attempt to segregate Black suffragists). The achievement was real; the framing of "we all won together in 1920" papers over what was actually fractured and exclusive.`,
                  insight: `The triumph framing captures real achievement but obscures racial exclusions within the movement and the additional 45 years of Black women's disenfranchisement after 1920.`,
