                  label: `Complicated victory: real achievement coexisted with racial exclusions, internal compromises, and limits that should be named honestly.`,
                  outcome: `Substantially closest to current scholarly consensus. Evidence: 19th Amendment was real and substantial achievement; it formally established white women's voting rights and opened space for subsequent advocacy; movement built sustained political organization across decades. But: the movement's internal racism is documented (Stanton's post-15th Amendment arguments; NAWSA's exclusion practices; 1913 parade segregation attempt); Black women suffragists built parallel organizations (Anna Julia Cooper, Ida B. Wells, Mary Church Terrell, Mary McLeod Bethune, Frances Ellen Watkins Harper); Black women in the South were substantially excluded from voting until Voting Rights Act 1965 (45 years after 19th Amendment). Honest engagement names both achievement and limits without dismissing either. Some scholars argue the movement's racial compromises were strategically necessary for white-women's suffrage to pass through Southern states; others argue the compromises substantially shaped what kind of feminism succeeded and which women's interests it would prioritize.`,
